{"abstract":"The dynamics of bacterial growth and concentrations of oxygen and carbon dioxide in the headspace of modified atmosphere packaged (MAP) meat is studied. The work is based on extensive long-time storage experiments on pork chops lasting up to 42 days at temperatures 0 °C and 5 °C and four different headspace gas mixtures with 10%, 20%, 40% and 70% oxygen and 30% carbon dioxide. The headspace dynamics is modelled. The model includes oxygen consuming biochemical processes in the meat and also the coupling between headspace and meat, specifically, the diffusion of oxygen and carbon dioxide into the meat. The model is parametrized from the experimental data, and captures the experimental results. It is concluded that the oxygen consuming processes inside the meat packed with MAP consume less than 1% of the initial oxygen in the headspace, and are not important for the headspace dynamics. On the other hand, the dissolution of carbon dioxide has a significant effect. This is due to the relatively large solubility of carbon dioxide compared to oxygen. From the model we find and characterize three distinct phases behind the headspace volume reduction observed. As the model is dynamical and mechanistic it can be used to give information about the retail-packed fresh meat under different oxygen containing atmospheres, effects of leaks, and more.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Tofteskov, Jon","Tørngren, Mari Ann","Bailey, Nicholas","Hansen, Jesper Schmidt"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-566659"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bacterial growth","MAP","Mathematical model","Oxygen Consumption"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-U"],"level3":{"GTS":["DI-GTS-ALL","DI-GTS-DTCI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-GTS-DTCI","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"modelling headspace dynamics in modified atmosphere packaged meat","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-566659","identifier":[{"id":"85059552760","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-9e77fda9-bfb7-4636-8a83-a625b07abab2"],"keyword":["Bacterial growth","Carbon dioxide dissolution","MAP","Mathematical model","Oxygen consumption"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BFECEC10B3AD3183AC08D47894F026A1"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of Science and Environment"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"danish technological institute","l2":"DI-GTS","l2n":"GTS Institutes","l3":"DI-GTS-DTCI","l3nn":"Danish Technological Institute","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Danish Technological Institute","namevar":["Danish Technological Institute"],"nora_code":"DI-GTS-DTCI","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jon","key":"tofteskovpau","last":"Tofteskov","locid":"bc442e41-5742-45a1-828a-a92f528316ea","name":"Tofteskov, Jon","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Mari Ann","key":"tørngrenpau","last":"Tørngren","name":"Tørngren, Mari Ann","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Nicholas","key":"baileypau","last":"Bailey","locid":"5dc0db10-4afc-4868-85f6-4872fbbc0686","name":"Bailey, Nicholas","orcid":"0000-0002-2519-2403","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jesper Schmidt","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"413f2c94-d35d-4ae4-89f2-361d8fc3ccc3","name":"Hansen, Jesper Schmidt","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jfoodeng.2018.12.013","issn":["02608774","18735770"],"issn_org":["02608774"],"journal":"Journal of Food Engineering","pages":"46-52","status":"p","title":"Journal of Food Engineering","volume":"248"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85059552760"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All GTS Institutes","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-GTS-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","GTS Institutes","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-9e77fda9-bfb7-4636-8a83-a625b07abab2"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Modelling headspace dynamics in modified atmosphere packaged meat","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-566659","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background: The effect of individual saturated fatty acids (SFAs) on serum cholesterol levels depends on their carbon-chain length. Whether the association with myocardial infarction (MI) also differs across individual SFAs is unclear. We examined the association between consumption of individual SFAs, differing in chain lengths ranging from 4 through 18 carbons, and risk of MI. Methods: We used data from 22,050 and 53,375 participants from EPIC-Norfolk (UK) and EPIC-Denmark, respectively. Baseline SFA intakes were assessed through validated, country-specific food frequency questionnaires. Cox regression analysis was used to estimate associations between intakes of individual SFAs and MI risk, for each cohort separately. Results: During median follow-up times of 18.8 years in EPIC-Norfolk and 13.6 years in Denmark, respectively, 1204 and 2260 MI events occurred. Mean (±SD) total SFA intake was 13.3 (±3.5) en% in EPIC-Norfolk, and 12.5 (±2.6) en% in EPIC-Denmark. After multivariable adjustment, intakes of C12:0 (lauric acid) and C14:0 (myristic acid) inversely associated with MI risk in EPIC-Denmark (HR upper versus lowest quintile: 0.80 (95%CI: 0.66, 0.96) for both SFAs). Intakes in the third and fourth quintiles of C4:0–C10:0 also associated with lower MI risk in EPIC-Denmark. Moreover, substitution of C16:0 (palmitic acid) and C18:0 (stearic acid) with plant proteins resulted in a reduction of MI risk in EPIC-Denmark (HR per 1 energy%: 0.86 (95%CI: 0.78, 0.95) and 0.87 (95%CI: 0.79, 0.96) respectively). No such associations were found in EPIC-Norfolk. Conclusion: The results from the present study suggest that the association between SFA and MI risk depends on the carbon chain-length of the SFA.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Praagman, Jaike","Vissers, Linda E.T.","Mulligan, Angela A.","Laursen, Anne Sofie Dam","Beulens, Joline W.J.","van der Schouw, Yvonne T.","Wareham, Nicholas J.","Hansen, Camilla Plambeck","Khaw, Kay Tee","Jakobsen, Marianne Uhre","Sluijs, Ivonne"],"cuid":["S02-099.82-025618"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Sluijs, Ivonne"],"country":["Netherlands","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Amsterdam UMC","University of Cambridge","Utrecht University"],"keyword":["Cohort study","Diet","Myocardial Infarction","Saturated fatty acids"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"consumption of individual saturated fatty acids and the risk of myocardial infarction in a uk and a danish cohort","src":["au","dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijcard.2018.10.064","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["EPIC-Norfolk: All authors report programme grants from Cancer Research UK ( C864/A8257 , C864/A14136 ) and the Medical Research Council (MRC) ( G0401527 , G1000143 ) during the study. NJW is also supported by the MRC (grant numbers MC_UU_12015/3 , MC_UU_12015/4 ). EPIC-Denmark: The primary data collection for the Diet, Cancer and Health cohort was funded by the Danish Cancer Society . JP is financially supported by a restricted Research Grant from Unilever Research and Development, Vlaardingen, the Netherlands; YTvdS and IS report grants from Unilever Research and Development, Vlaardingen, the Netherlands. None of the other authors declares a conflict of interest. Appendix A"],"id":"S02-099.82-025618","identifier":[{"id":"85055468887","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC6774776","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"30482628","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-b18c9d87-ac45-4fbd-91a2-0a0b1a450ed4","dtu-3e0ea30e-84d2-4fe9-b6e4-9a