{"abstract":"We used genetic variants that are robustly associated with adiposity to examine the causal association of adiposity with psychological distress.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Lawlor, Debbie A","Harbord, Roger M","Tybjærg-Hansen, Anne","Palmer, Tom M","Zacho, Jeppe","Benn, Marianne","Timpson, Nicholas J","Smith, George Davey","Nordestgaard, Børge G","Benn, Marianne"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-395155"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"using genetic loci to understand the relationship between adiposity and psychological distress a mendelian randomization study in the copenhagen general population study of 000000000053 000000000221 adults","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2796.2011.02343.x","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-395155","identifier":[{"id":"79954605539","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-02d1c532-d7e6-4b65-aad3-4dfe2056f3c9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-219D0CBBF47649DFAB293B3EFAC63FE3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2796.2011.02343.x","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":1,"key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Section of Diagnostic Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU"}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Debbie A","key":"lawlordebbieapau","last":"Lawlor","name":"Lawlor, Debbie A","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Roger M","key":"harbordrogermpau","last":"Harbord","name":"Harbord, Roger M","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Anne","key":"tybjærghansenannepau","last":"Tybjærg-Hansen","locid":"452a492e-c869-41e5-b60e-1d2a542287cf","name":"Tybjærg-Hansen, Anne","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Tom M","key":"palmertommpau","last":"Palmer","name":"Palmer, Tom M","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jeppe","key":"zachojeppepau","last":"Zacho","name":"Zacho, Jeppe","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marianne","key":"bennmariannepau","last":"Benn","name":"Benn, Marianne","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Nicholas J","key":"timpsonnicholasjpau","last":"Timpson","name":"Timpson, Nicholas J","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"George Davey","key":"smithgeorgedaveypau","last":"Smith","name":"Smith, George Davey","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Børge G","key":"nordestgaardbørgegpau","last":"Nordestgaard","locid":"733103b5-ddc2-416f-a686-48e694154a82","name":"Nordestgaard, Børge G","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/j.1365-2796.2011.02343.x","issn":["09546820","09557873"],"issn_org":["09546820"],"issue":"5","journal":"Journal of Internal Medicine","pages":"525-37","status":"p","title":"Journal of Internal Medicine","volume":"269"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-79954605539"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-02d1c532-d7e6-4b65-aad3-4dfe2056f3c9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Using genetic loci to understand the relationship between adiposity and psychological distress: a Mendelian Randomization study in the Copenhagen General Population Study of 53,221 adults","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-395155","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"Background The FTO gene harbors the strongest known susceptibility locus for obesity. While many individual studies have suggested that physical activity (PA) may attenuate the effect of FTO on obesity risk, other studies have not been able to confirm this interaction. To confirm or refute unambiguously whether PA attenuates the association of FTO with obesity risk, we meta-analyzed data from 45 studies of adults (n = 218,166) and nine studies of children and adolescents (n = 19,268). Methods and Findings All studies identified to have data on the FTO rs9939609 variant (or any proxy [r2>0.8]) and PA were invited to participate, regardless of ethnicity or age of the participants. PA was standardized by categorizing it into a dichotomous variable (physically inactive versus active) in each study. Overall, 25% of adults and 13% of children were categorized as inactive. Interaction analyses were performed within each study by including the FTO×PA interaction term in an additive model, adjusting for age and sex. Subsequently, random effects meta-analysis was used to pool the interaction terms. In adults, the minor (A-) allele of rs9939609 increased the odds of obesity by 1.23-fold/allele (95% CI 1.20–1.26), but PA attenuated this effect (pinteraction = 0.001). More specifically, the minor allele of rs9939609 increased the odds of obesity less in the physically active group (odds ratio = 1.22/allele, 95% CI 1.19–1.25) than in the inactive group (odds ratio = 1.30/allele, 95% CI 1.24–1.36). No such interaction was found in children and adolescents. Conclusions The association of the FTO risk allele with the odds of obesity is attenuated by 27% in physically active adults, highlighting the importance of PA in particular in those genetically predisposed to obesity.","author_count":119,"contributors":["Kilpeläinen, Tuomas O","Qi, Lu","Brage, Soren","Sharp, Stephen J","Sonestedt, Emily","Demerath, Ellen","Ahmad, Tariq","Mora, Samia","Kaakinen, Marika","Sandholt, C